00:10:05.450 --> 00:10:07.040
Now for those of
you who said, hey,

00:10:07.040 --> 00:10:09.460
you're still early in
considering Windows Server

00:10:09.460 --> 00:10:11.260
containers, here's a
very quick overview

00:10:11.260 --> 00:10:12.610
of Windows Server containers.

00:10:12.610 --> 00:10:15.100
For those of you who've
been doing this for a while,

00:10:15.100 --> 00:10:17.900
this should be all
obviously very familiar.

00:10:17.900 --> 00:10:19.930
It's often good to
think of containers

00:10:19.930 --> 00:10:22.480
in the context of virtual
machines if you're new to it.

00:10:22.480 --> 00:10:25.090
On the left hand side is what
I presume everyone in the room

00:10:25.090 --> 00:10:26.200
is familiar with, right?

00:10:26.200 --> 00:10:27.610
Virtual machines.

00:10:27.610 --> 00:10:29.980
Typically, virtually
machines were

00:10:29.980 --> 00:10:31.900
invented to virtualize
the underlying hardware

00:10:31.900 --> 00:10:34.690
or the infrastructure so
much so that each of the OS

00:10:34.690 --> 00:10:37.480
and the application running
on top of the OS pretty much

00:10:37.480 --> 00:10:39.550
thinks that it has the
hardware to itself.

00:10:39.550 --> 00:10:43.030
And that was the
technology of hypervisor.

00:10:43.030 --> 00:10:45.050
Now containers is
somewhat similar.

00:10:45.050 --> 00:10:47.470
So it's good to think of
containers in that context

00:10:47.470 --> 00:10:50.230
where the application and
the dependent binaries

00:10:50.230 --> 00:10:52.260
that the application
depends on essentially

00:10:52.260 --> 00:10:54.760
is the container, the red box
that you see on the right hand

00:10:54.760 --> 00:10:55.510
side.

00:10:55.510 --> 00:10:57.460
And those, of course,
many of those containers

00:10:57.460 --> 00:11:01.360
can run inside of a
virtual machine in GCP.

00:11:01.360 --> 00:11:05.050
Essentially, containers are
virtualizing the underlying

00:11:05.050 --> 00:11:07.540
operating system so much so
that the application thinks

00:11:07.540 --> 00:11:09.880
that it has OS to itself
whereas in reality, we all

00:11:09.880 --> 00:11:10.490
know it's not.

00:11:10.490 --> 00:11:12.580
So that's kind of like,
you know, quick overview

00:11:12.580 --> 00:11:14.120
of what containers are.

00:11:14.120 --> 00:11:16.380
And in the world of
Windows Server, containers,

00:11:16.380 --> 00:11:18.427
they're very similar
to Linux containers.

00:11:18.427 --> 00:11:20.260
Of course, there are a
couple of differences

00:11:20.260 --> 00:11:22.622
that we'll kind of highlight
in the next few slides.

00:11:22.622 --> 00:11:24.330
But you want to think
of a Windows Server

00:11:24.330 --> 00:11:27.670
container on the right
hand side as consisting

00:11:27.670 --> 00:11:29.447
of several layers.

00:11:29.447 --> 00:11:31.030
Typically inside of
a virtual machine,

00:11:31.030 --> 00:11:33.238
when you're running a Windows
Server container that's

00:11:33.238 --> 00:11:36.460
in the red box, there is,
of course, a host OS, right?

00:11:36.460 --> 00:11:38.860
That's a Windows Server
host OS that's running.

00:11:38.860 --> 00:11:41.830
On top of that, there's a
container runtime like Docker.

00:11:41.830 --> 00:11:44.347
And on top of that runs your
Windows Server container.

00:11:44.347 --> 00:11:46.180
And a container, a
Windows Server container,

00:11:46.180 --> 00:11:49.090
like you can see here, is
composed of multiple layers.

00:11:49.090 --> 00:11:51.310
Typically, you know,
there's a base OS layer

00:11:51.310 --> 00:11:52.810
image at the bottom, obviously.

00:11:52.810 --> 00:11:54.935
And above that, there are
a number of custom layers

00:11:54.935 --> 00:11:55.990
that you can add on top.

00:11:55.990 --> 00:11:58.930
It can be IIS, Partial, et
cetera, et cetera, right?

00:11:58.930 --> 00:12:02.050
A lot of things you can add
on top of the base OS layer.

00:12:02.050 --> 00:12:04.300
And then on top of that
sits your application.

00:12:04.300 --> 00:12:07.030
That, in a sense, is really
how you make containers, right?

00:12:07.030 --> 00:12:08.593
windows Server containers.

00:12:08.593 --> 00:12:10.760
Hopefully that was a quick
overview for those of you

00:12:10.760 --> 00:12:15.620
who were early in the Windows
Server containers land.

00:12:15.620 --> 00:12:18.590
Now as you make choice of
your Windows Server containers

00:12:18.590 --> 00:12:21.410
and you make those containers
of your applications,

00:12:21.410 --> 00:12:24.570
a couple of things I wanted to
kind of run by very quickly.

00:12:24.570 --> 00:12:27.140
The first thing you'll be doing
is selecting your underlying

00:12:27.140 --> 00:12:28.190
host OS.

00:12:28.190 --> 00:12:30.600
Now as many of you may
know, from Microsoft,

00:12:30.600 --> 00:12:32.450
there are two releases
or two channels

00:12:32.450 --> 00:12:35.240
that Microsoft has for
Windows Server, one

00:12:35.240 --> 00:12:37.110
they call Long Term
Service Channel,

00:12:37.110 --> 00:12:39.260
and the other is called
semi-annual Channel.

00:12:39.260 --> 00:12:43.770
LTSC, examples of that are
Windows Server 2016, 2019,

00:12:43.770 --> 00:12:44.540
and so on.

00:12:44.540 --> 00:12:48.210
And of course, on a semi-annual
channel release schedule,

00:12:48.210 --> 00:12:50.330
these are released
every six months or so.

00:12:50.330 --> 00:12:52.370
So the latest one is 18.09.

00:12:52.370 --> 00:12:54.350
And usually, the
numbering or the

00:12:54.350 --> 00:12:56.880
build numbering for that
follows the year and the month

00:12:56.880 --> 00:12:57.630
after that, right?

00:12:57.630 --> 00:13:00.233
So that's the key difference
between those two releases.

00:13:00.233 --> 00:13:01.900
A couple of quick
things to note there--

00:13:01.900 --> 00:13:03.660
if you are looking
into containers,

00:13:03.660 --> 00:13:05.420
I'll point out the
support that Microsoft

00:13:05.420 --> 00:13:07.610
provides for either of them
is a little bit different.

00:13:07.610 --> 00:13:09.735
So you want to make your
choice for underlying host

00:13:09.735 --> 00:13:12.410
OS based on that if
that's of importance.

00:13:12.410 --> 00:13:14.210
But that said, there
is a lot changing

00:13:14.210 --> 00:13:15.740
with the server containers.

00:13:15.740 --> 00:13:17.270
In fact, updates
are being released

00:13:17.270 --> 00:13:18.870
on a very frequent basis.

00:13:18.870 --> 00:13:21.050
So the recommendation
from Microsoft

00:13:21.050 --> 00:13:23.720
is obviously to use
the semiannual channel

00:13:23.720 --> 00:13:25.720
for your container
applications because then you

00:13:25.720 --> 00:13:27.928
can keep up to date with
all of that good tech that's

00:13:27.928 --> 00:13:30.190
coming out and rolling into
these semi-annual updates,

00:13:30.190 --> 00:13:30.710
right?

00:13:30.710 --> 00:13:33.210
So that's kind of a little bit
about choosing the underlying

00:13:33.210 --> 00:13:34.990
host OS.

00:13:34.990 --> 00:13:37.130
Now going one layer
up, as you are choosing

00:13:37.130 --> 00:13:40.220
your next layer of your
Windows Server container,

00:13:40.220 --> 00:13:43.070
you have to make a choice
on what kind of a base image

00:13:43.070 --> 00:13:45.170
you want inside
of your container.

00:13:45.170 --> 00:13:47.180
There are two broad
types of base images

00:13:47.180 --> 00:13:49.430
that you can choose from--
the Windows Server Core

00:13:49.430 --> 00:13:52.280
and the Windows Nano Server.

00:13:52.280 --> 00:13:55.970
The key differences between them
are, of course, one is of size.

00:13:55.970 --> 00:13:58.250
For those of you who may
know, Windows Server Core,

00:13:58.250 --> 00:14:00.720
when you download from the
registry, is a little over two

00:14:00.720 --> 00:14:01.220
gig.

00:14:01.220 --> 00:14:04.820
And of course, uncompressed,
it can go up to about five gig,

00:14:04.820 --> 00:14:05.900
right?

00:14:05.900 --> 00:14:08.660
Windows Nano Server, on the
other hand, is very tiny,

00:14:08.660 --> 00:14:11.470
almost compares to
Linux container sizes.

00:14:11.470 --> 00:14:13.340
When you download,
that's under 100 meg now,

00:14:13.340 --> 00:14:15.132
and of course, when
you explode that up by,

00:14:15.132 --> 00:14:17.560
it's a little bit over 200 meg.

00:14:17.560 --> 00:14:20.030
Now the scenarios where
you'll be choosing either

00:14:20.030 --> 00:14:23.900
or are going to be dependent
on your applications.

00:14:23.900 --> 00:14:26.240
If you want a lot of
compatibility for your legacy

00:14:26.240 --> 00:14:27.800
applications with
the older stuff,

00:14:27.800 --> 00:14:29.540
you want to go for Server Core.

00:14:29.540 --> 00:14:33.710
Of course, a lot of the new
applications like .NET core,

00:14:33.710 --> 00:14:38.170
IIS, ASP.NET, et cetera, will
do just fine in Nano Server.

00:14:38.170 --> 00:14:40.940
The usual rule of thumb
is go with Nano Server.

00:14:40.940 --> 00:14:43.340
If for some reason,
compatible reason, you cannot,

00:14:43.340 --> 00:14:45.200
of course go down
the left side, right,

00:14:45.200 --> 00:14:46.642
with Windows Server Core.

00:14:46.642 --> 00:14:48.350
So hopefully that's
a little bit of a tip

00:14:48.350 --> 00:14:53.348
on choosing the next layer for
your Windows Server containers.

00:14:53.348 --> 00:14:55.640
Now one of the things that
is kind of like a little bit

00:14:55.640 --> 00:14:57.110
different in the world
of Windows Server

00:14:57.110 --> 00:14:59.480
containers versus what you
may have been used with Linux

00:14:59.480 --> 00:15:02.600
containers is that you
must make sure that you

00:15:02.600 --> 00:15:06.650
have to match the host and
the container OS versions.

00:15:06.650 --> 00:15:08.483
So you know, this is
just a bit of a gotcha,

00:15:08.483 --> 00:15:10.858
if you're especially coming
from Linux world, to kind of,

00:15:10.858 --> 00:15:12.470
like, make sure those two match.

00:15:12.470 --> 00:15:14.450
And when it comes to
Windows Server numbering

00:15:14.450 --> 00:15:16.850
system for versions, there
are four numbers, right?

00:15:16.850 --> 00:15:20.653
There is major, minor,
build, and revision.

00:15:20.653 --> 00:15:22.820
Now the first three are the
ones that have to match.

00:15:22.820 --> 00:15:24.772
The last one, it's OK
for it not to match.

00:15:24.772 --> 00:15:26.480
The revision number
is typically a number

00:15:26.480 --> 00:15:30.140
that gets updated every time you
apply a patch, like a KB patch,

00:15:30.140 --> 00:15:31.490
to your image.

00:15:31.490 --> 00:15:32.960
So that's the key difference.

00:15:32.960 --> 00:15:34.560
Just to illustrate
a quick example,

00:15:34.560 --> 00:15:36.930
let's say if you have a
container image and host OS

00:15:36.930 --> 00:15:37.430
version.

00:15:37.430 --> 00:15:39.920
And those are the versions
that they're running.

00:15:39.920 --> 00:15:42.950
If you end up, let's
say, upgrading or rather

00:15:42.950 --> 00:15:45.530
applying a patch update
to the host OS version

00:15:45.530 --> 00:15:47.960
and you don't update or
apply that same patch

00:15:47.960 --> 00:15:50.910
to your container image,
it'll just work fine.

00:15:50.910 --> 00:15:53.840
However, if you upgrade
the underlying host OS,

00:15:53.840 --> 00:15:55.790
let's say, to next
build and you do not

00:15:55.790 --> 00:15:58.190
do that for the container,
it just won't run, right?

00:15:58.190 --> 00:15:59.425
Like, it's not even now--

00:15:59.425 --> 00:16:01.070
it just won't even start, right?

00:16:01.070 --> 00:16:02.810
So just kind of like something
to remember as you're

00:16:02.810 --> 00:16:04.130
thinking about what
version to choose

00:16:04.130 --> 00:16:06.650
and how to mix and match your
order sort of the container

00:16:06.650 --> 00:16:08.552
and host versions.

00:16:08.552 --> 00:16:10.010
Now we get a lot
of these questions

00:16:10.010 --> 00:16:12.590
from customers also
on licensing, right?

00:16:12.590 --> 00:16:15.470
How does licensing work
for Windows containers?

00:16:15.470 --> 00:16:19.100
So I just wanted to spend a
minute on that now the way

00:16:19.100 --> 00:16:21.860
Microsoft treats
container licenses is

00:16:21.860 --> 00:16:25.650
that they are supplements to
the underlying host OS licenses.

00:16:25.650 --> 00:16:28.160
What that basically means
is if you are running

00:16:28.160 --> 00:16:30.770
a licensed holster
with inside of OEM,

00:16:30.770 --> 00:16:32.030
you can run containers, right?

00:16:32.030 --> 00:16:33.980
Your license actually
covers for that.

00:16:33.980 --> 00:16:37.190
So the good news is in GCE,
as long as you're spinning up

00:16:37.190 --> 00:16:39.463
your Windows Server
VMs, you're already

00:16:39.463 --> 00:16:40.880
licensed for because
you're paying

00:16:40.880 --> 00:16:42.290
for the license
on the underlying

00:16:42.290 --> 00:16:43.670
sort of virtual machine.

00:16:43.670 --> 00:16:46.680
You can run any number
of containers on top.

00:16:46.680 --> 00:16:49.670
And because Windows Server
2019 Data Center and Standard

00:16:49.670 --> 00:16:52.160
Editions allow you to run
unlimited number of Windows

00:16:52.160 --> 00:16:54.470
Server containers,
there's a ton of savings

00:16:54.470 --> 00:16:56.810
you can actually
get by packing a lot

00:16:56.810 --> 00:16:59.375
of those containers on one OS.

00:16:59.375 --> 00:17:01.250
The other key point I
want to sort of mention

00:17:01.250 --> 00:17:06.020
here is if you are running
containers on top of a host OS,

00:17:06.020 --> 00:17:08.069
you can only do that
for running containers.

00:17:08.069 --> 00:17:09.800
So you can't have
some containers

00:17:09.800 --> 00:17:12.560
and have something
else run on top also.

00:17:12.560 --> 00:17:15.180
That's what on the right side
of the slide it says, right?

00:17:15.180 --> 00:17:17.670
It has to be used solely
to run containers.

00:17:17.670 --> 00:17:20.290
So that's kind of like a
licensing term as well.

00:17:20.290 --> 00:17:23.619
So hopefully some tips
here on licensing.

00:17:23.619 --> 00:17:26.078
Now of course, we get a lot
of questions from customers.

00:17:26.078 --> 00:17:28.620
Many of you are thinking about
how to basically containerize,

00:17:28.620 --> 00:17:30.360
what to containerize, what not.

00:17:30.360 --> 00:17:33.327
Those are all great
discussions, and it's

00:17:33.327 --> 00:17:35.160
hard to come up with a
prescriptive guidance

00:17:35.160 --> 00:17:36.963
for that sort of a
question, honestly.

00:17:36.963 --> 00:17:38.880
Just now you heard from
Alex as to the journey

00:17:38.880 --> 00:17:40.680
that they have
embarked on, especially

00:17:40.680 --> 00:17:42.540
as they sort of modernized
and containerized

00:17:42.540 --> 00:17:43.770
their applications.

00:17:43.770 --> 00:17:46.440
And your journey could be very
similar or very different,

00:17:46.440 --> 00:17:47.190
honestly.

00:17:47.190 --> 00:17:49.295
In many cases, what I do
recommend to customers

00:17:49.295 --> 00:17:50.670
is that have a
bit of a checklist

00:17:50.670 --> 00:17:52.530
that you prepare
for applications

00:17:52.530 --> 00:17:55.120
and have some criteria by which
you assess those applications,

00:17:55.120 --> 00:17:57.130
whether they are
containerizable or not.

00:17:57.130 --> 00:17:59.860
And some, of course, tips
are here on the slide.

00:17:59.860 --> 00:18:02.320
Great candidates for
containerization generally are,

00:18:02.320 --> 00:18:04.440
you know, your web
and logic tiers,

00:18:04.440 --> 00:18:07.170
in-house .NET apps if you've got
custom apps that you developed

00:18:07.170 --> 00:18:09.628
yourself because you know the
code and you can containerize

00:18:09.628 --> 00:18:12.390
them and, of course, slightly
more recent applications that

00:18:12.390 --> 00:18:17.607
are perhaps IIS 6 Plus or 3.5
.NET framework and onwards.

00:18:17.607 --> 00:18:19.440
And, of course, new
applications that you're

00:18:19.440 --> 00:18:21.420
developing using
Microsoft's architectures

00:18:21.420 --> 00:18:23.040
and/or cloud native
applications also

00:18:23.040 --> 00:18:24.960
make for great candidates
for containers.

00:18:24.960 --> 00:18:26.807
Now some of those
applications that you

00:18:26.807 --> 00:18:28.890
want to stay away from for
a little bit, at least,

00:18:28.890 --> 00:18:31.950
is any application that
has UI experience, right,

00:18:31.950 --> 00:18:33.600
because containers
won't give you that.

00:18:33.600 --> 00:18:37.055
RDP, VDI are examples kind of
like to stay away from for now.

00:18:37.055 --> 00:18:39.180
And especially if there is
an underlying dependency

00:18:39.180 --> 00:18:42.360
your application has on GPUs
or TPUs or any low level

00:18:42.360 --> 00:18:45.440
networking constructs out
there, you want to kind of look

00:18:45.440 --> 00:18:47.190
at it a little bit
more closely before you

00:18:47.190 --> 00:18:48.660
decide to containerize.

00:18:48.660 --> 00:18:50.220
And of course, with
AD integration,

00:18:50.220 --> 00:18:51.762
that's something
that's in the works.

00:18:51.762 --> 00:18:55.350
So I would say keep
looking out for that.

00:18:55.350 --> 00:18:57.330
Upstream Kubernetes has
that in alpha support,

00:18:57.330 --> 00:19:00.070
and of course, we will have
plans to support that as well.

00:19:00.070 --> 00:19:02.370
But that's something to
absolutely keep on lookout for.

00:19:02.370 --> 00:19:05.207
One thing I'll mention
here is that any

00:19:05.207 --> 00:19:07.040
of the things that are
important to you that

00:19:07.040 --> 00:19:09.665
are on the right hand side, due
talk to us because the world is

00:19:09.665 --> 00:19:10.830
changing very quickly.

00:19:10.830 --> 00:19:13.530
We're adding a ton of innovation
as is Microsoft as well

00:19:13.530 --> 00:19:14.213
with containers.

00:19:14.213 --> 00:19:15.880
So we're all listening
to your feedback.

00:19:15.880 --> 00:19:19.610
So keep talking to us.

00:19:19.610 --> 00:19:21.800
Now outside of that,
in Google Cloud

00:19:21.800 --> 00:19:23.840
we've been, of course,
supporting Windows Server

00:19:23.840 --> 00:19:24.950
containers for a while.

00:19:24.950 --> 00:19:28.475
If you go to your management
console and open up the menu,

00:19:28.475 --> 00:19:30.350
there are a number of
images already that you

00:19:30.350 --> 00:19:31.850
can get started with today.

00:19:31.850 --> 00:19:33.740
And if you choose one
of these container

00:19:33.740 --> 00:19:36.003
optimized images
for Windows Server,

00:19:36.003 --> 00:19:37.920
they come in with Docker
pre-installed, right?

00:19:37.920 --> 00:19:39.795
So you don't have to
install Docker yourself.

00:19:39.795 --> 00:19:41.660
They are ready to go.

00:19:41.660 --> 00:19:43.910
And they have Server Core,
Nano Server images already

00:19:43.910 --> 00:19:46.370
downloaded and loaded up in
the local registry inside

00:19:46.370 --> 00:19:47.520
of those virtual machines.

00:19:47.520 --> 00:19:49.070
So if you want to give
it a spin immediately,

00:19:49.070 --> 00:19:51.445
you can do so without having
to download them on the wire

00:19:51.445 --> 00:19:52.130
again.

00:19:52.130 --> 00:19:55.077
And then, of course, GCP does
have a cloud registry for you

00:19:55.077 --> 00:19:57.660
to kind of make modifications,
if you want, to those container

00:19:57.660 --> 00:19:59.810
images, push and
pull those images

00:19:59.810 --> 00:20:01.670
at your will, which
of course is going

00:20:01.670 --> 00:20:04.880
to be a lot more important as
you look at Kubernetes and GKE,

00:20:04.880 --> 00:20:06.980
of course, as well
for the applications.

00:20:06.980 --> 00:20:09.230
And if you're interested in
learning a little bit more

00:20:09.230 --> 00:20:12.238
about how do you take a .NET
application, containerize it,

00:20:12.238 --> 00:20:14.780
et cetera, there is a session
that we're running later today.

00:20:14.780 --> 00:20:16.520
We'll go into a little
bit more of depth

00:20:16.520 --> 00:20:19.710
and show a demo
as well for that.

00:20:19.710 --> 00:20:21.810
Now, obviously that
was a whirlwind tour

00:20:21.810 --> 00:20:24.380
of hopefully Windows Server
containers and hopefully

00:20:24.380 --> 00:20:26.090
some tips and tricks
for those of you

00:20:26.090 --> 00:20:28.605
who are very early in
the journey with us.

00:20:28.605 --> 00:20:30.980
Now let's shift our attention
to talk about orchestration

00:20:30.980 --> 00:20:31.945
with Kubernetes.

00:20:31.945 --> 00:20:34.070
Y-Ju will be covering a
little bit about Kubernetes

00:20:34.070 --> 00:20:37.040
and then talking about our
plans to support it in GKE.

00:20:37.040 --> 00:20:37.540
Yu-Ju?

00:20:40.174 --> 00:20:40.674
Sorry.

00:20:46.265 --> 00:20:47.640
YU-JU HONG: Yeah,
thanks, Venkat.

00:20:47.640 --> 00:20:49.980
So in the next part,
I'll be walking you

00:20:49.980 --> 00:20:52.650
through a high level overview
of Windows Server containers

00:20:52.650 --> 00:20:55.020
with Kubernetes
and then with GKE.

00:20:55.020 --> 00:20:56.880
And then I'll wrap
it up with a demo.

00:21:01.540 --> 00:21:04.000
So if you don't know
about Kubernetes already,

00:21:04.000 --> 00:21:07.670
it's the leading container
orchestration system out there.

00:21:07.670 --> 00:21:10.690
It helps you automate
deployment of your applications,

00:21:10.690 --> 00:21:12.910
managing your life
cycles, and scaling

00:21:12.910 --> 00:21:16.670
your applications as needed.

00:21:16.670 --> 00:21:20.500
So this technology was actually
inspired by Google's experience

00:21:20.500 --> 00:21:22.210
of running billions
of containers

00:21:22.210 --> 00:21:24.310
internally over the years.

00:21:24.310 --> 00:21:26.520
And it's completely
the open source

00:21:26.520 --> 00:21:27.880
and runs in multiple clouds.

00:21:31.090 --> 00:21:35.350
So here's an oversimplified
view of Kubernetes architecture.

00:21:35.350 --> 00:21:37.510
At the bottom, you
have the worker nodes,

00:21:37.510 --> 00:21:39.520
which are typically
virtually machines

00:21:39.520 --> 00:21:41.080
running your workloads.

00:21:41.080 --> 00:21:43.270
And then you have the
master control plane

00:21:43.270 --> 00:21:46.720
at the top that includes
API servers, scheduler,

00:21:46.720 --> 00:21:49.060
and various controllers.

00:21:49.060 --> 00:21:53.410
So a Kubernetes user would just
write a workload specification

00:21:53.410 --> 00:21:55.570
indicating how many
replicas they want,

00:21:55.570 --> 00:21:58.030
what kind of container
images to use, and then

00:21:58.030 --> 00:22:02.320
submit this spec to
the API server, which

00:22:02.320 --> 00:22:05.950
will get dispatched eventually
onto the worker nodes.

00:22:05.950 --> 00:22:08.890
And then we have the per
node agent, which is kubelet,

00:22:08.890 --> 00:22:10.690
that will start
off the containers

00:22:10.690 --> 00:22:11.890
and manage them for you.

00:22:15.080 --> 00:22:18.540
So Kubernetes started with
Linux containers only.

00:22:18.540 --> 00:22:20.870
But in the past two
years, the community

00:22:20.870 --> 00:22:23.300
has been working
closely with Microsoft

00:22:23.300 --> 00:22:25.910
to support Windows containers.

00:22:25.910 --> 00:22:28.340
So I'm happy to
tell you that just

00:22:28.340 --> 00:22:32.480
in the last month in the
Kubernetes 1.14 release,

00:22:32.480 --> 00:22:34.950
Windows Server containers
running on Windows nodes

00:22:34.950 --> 00:22:38.970
were declared as
a stable feature.

00:22:38.970 --> 00:22:42.630
So kudos to all the community
members who helped to make this

00:22:42.630 --> 00:22:43.130
happen.

00:22:50.650 --> 00:22:56.370
So how do Windows Server
containers work in Kubernetes?

00:22:56.370 --> 00:22:58.680
So let's see an
overview of this.

00:22:58.680 --> 00:23:01.320
So here, we have the
architecture again.

00:23:01.320 --> 00:23:03.450
And you have the
master control plane.

00:23:03.450 --> 00:23:06.690
So this master control plane
will still run on the Linux.

00:23:06.690 --> 00:23:09.630
Currently, there's no plan
to make it run on Windows.

00:23:09.630 --> 00:23:11.740
But then you can
add Windows nodes.

00:23:11.740 --> 00:23:13.650
So those worker
nodes will be running

00:23:13.650 --> 00:23:17.910
Windows Server 2019, which
is a requirement with a 1.14

00:23:17.910 --> 00:23:19.380
release.

00:23:19.380 --> 00:23:21.050
And then on those
nodes, again, we

00:23:21.050 --> 00:23:22.680
will have per node
agents running.

00:23:26.530 --> 00:23:29.890
So with the Windows nodes,
now you can run Windows pods.

00:23:29.890 --> 00:23:34.030
So pod is composed of
multiple containers.

00:23:34.030 --> 00:23:38.110
It's a basic scheduling
unit in Kubernetes.

00:23:38.110 --> 00:23:43.150
So containers in a pod
share a single IP address.

00:23:43.150 --> 00:23:47.050
They can share volumes to
access the same set of data.

00:23:47.050 --> 00:23:49.300
And users can
customize their pods

00:23:49.300 --> 00:23:53.740
to use custom hooks
or health checks.

00:23:53.740 --> 00:23:58.120
And also, a pod can access
config maps or secrets which

00:23:58.120 --> 00:24:00.940
are Kubernetes concepts
for configurations

00:24:00.940 --> 00:24:04.087
or storing of
sensitive information.

00:24:04.087 --> 00:24:05.920
And then, of course,
you can view the status

00:24:05.920 --> 00:24:09.220
and getting the events and
metrics for your Windows pods

00:24:09.220 --> 00:24:10.940
as you can do for
your Linux pods.

00:24:15.580 --> 00:24:18.810
So with pods, now you
can build services.

00:24:18.810 --> 00:24:21.790
A service in Kubernetes
groups multiple pods

00:24:21.790 --> 00:24:23.860
with a defined access policy.

00:24:23.860 --> 00:24:26.150
For example, as
shown on the slide,

00:24:26.150 --> 00:24:29.740
you can have a service
with a single IP address.

00:24:29.740 --> 00:24:32.590
And then you can actually
have three different pods

00:24:32.590 --> 00:24:34.960
to back the service.

00:24:34.960 --> 00:24:38.150
So the traffic would get
distributed onto those three

00:24:38.150 --> 00:24:40.120
pods.

00:24:40.120 --> 00:24:43.360
So Kubernetes supports multiple
types of services or load

00:24:43.360 --> 00:24:48.550
balancers, and we support those
for both Windows and Linux

00:24:48.550 --> 00:24:49.270
pods.

00:24:49.270 --> 00:24:52.150
So this provides
cross-platform connectivity.

00:24:55.650 --> 00:24:58.240
So we talk about pod, which
is a very basic concept

00:24:58.240 --> 00:24:59.580
in Kubernetes.

00:24:59.580 --> 00:25:02.130
But most users don't
create pods directly.

00:25:02.130 --> 00:25:04.440
They create a higher level
construct that better

00:25:04.440 --> 00:25:06.280
suits their work loads.

00:25:06.280 --> 00:25:11.370
For example, a DaemonSet
would create a pod per node.

00:25:11.370 --> 00:25:13.320
Or If you use Job,
it will ensure

00:25:13.320 --> 00:25:17.850
that a number of desired
pause would run to completion.

00:25:17.850 --> 00:25:19.350
And then we have
StatefulSet, which

00:25:19.350 --> 00:25:22.200
provides stable network
and storage identity

00:25:22.200 --> 00:25:24.820
for each pod in the set.

00:25:24.820 --> 00:25:27.270
This is particularly
useful if you are

00:25:27.270 --> 00:25:29.100
accessing a persistent storage.

00:25:29.100 --> 00:25:31.920
And if the pod crashes or
if you recreate the pod,

00:25:31.920 --> 00:25:35.160
you want the pod to
assume the same identity

00:25:35.160 --> 00:25:36.780
and access the same
piece of storage.

00:25:39.960 --> 00:25:42.610
So these are all supported
for Windows pods as well.

00:25:45.540 --> 00:25:49.260
So we've only touched upon the
basic concepts in Kubernetes

00:25:49.260 --> 00:25:51.950
and what's supported
for Windows containers.

00:25:51.950 --> 00:25:54.540
There are so many other features
that are already supported

00:25:54.540 --> 00:25:56.370
for Windows containers.

00:25:56.370 --> 00:25:58.950
And again, there would be
some features that do not

00:25:58.950 --> 00:26:01.380
work for Windows pods yet.

00:26:01.380 --> 00:26:03.990
And it could be due to
the inherent limitation

00:26:03.990 --> 00:26:08.310
on Windows kernel and
the platform or just

00:26:08.310 --> 00:26:12.280
because a concept does not
exist on Windows at all.

00:26:12.280 --> 00:26:15.790
And you will find in some
cases, even though a feature

00:26:15.790 --> 00:26:20.110
does work for Windows,
the behavior on Windows

00:26:20.110 --> 00:26:23.470
pods, there might be a
slight behavioral difference

00:26:23.470 --> 00:26:26.060
between Windows
pods and Linux pods.

00:26:26.060 --> 00:26:29.010
So there are things
to watch out for.

00:26:29.010 --> 00:26:31.650
And of course, the
community is continuing

00:26:31.650 --> 00:26:33.530
working on rolling out--

00:26:33.530 --> 00:26:35.070
supporting different features.

00:26:35.070 --> 00:26:38.280
And many more will be coming
in the next few releases.

00:26:38.280 --> 00:26:41.370
So that's one incentive to
keep your Kubernetes cluster up

00:26:41.370 --> 00:26:42.773
to date.

00:26:42.773 --> 00:26:44.190
So if you are
curious and you want

00:26:44.190 --> 00:26:46.990
to find out more about what
features are supported,

00:26:46.990 --> 00:26:49.740
you can check out the
link on the slide.

00:26:49.740 --> 00:26:52.170
This is a documentation
maintained by the Kubernetes

00:26:52.170 --> 00:26:52.890
community.

00:26:52.890 --> 00:26:56.720
It will get updated frequently.

00:26:56.720 --> 00:26:59.250
Or if you don't
trust documentation,

00:26:59.250 --> 00:27:01.370
we also have continuous testing.

00:27:01.370 --> 00:27:04.370
This is our open source
testing dashboard

00:27:04.370 --> 00:27:07.460
that's running Kubernetes
on Google Compute Engine

00:27:07.460 --> 00:27:09.230
for Windows Server containers.

00:27:09.230 --> 00:27:12.170
It runs regularly
every couple of hours,

00:27:12.170 --> 00:27:14.630
and you can clearly see
what's working and what's not.

00:27:19.850 --> 00:27:22.710
So Kubernetes is great,
and many people love it.

00:27:22.710 --> 00:27:24.660
And it's widely used.

00:27:24.660 --> 00:27:27.450
But a lot of users want
to focus on developing

00:27:27.450 --> 00:27:32.160
their applications, not
managing the Kubernetes cluster,

00:27:32.160 --> 00:27:35.650
figuring out the operational
logistics day to day.

00:27:35.650 --> 00:27:37.790
And that's where GKE comes in.

00:27:37.790 --> 00:27:42.450
GKE provides a fully
managed master control plan.

00:27:42.450 --> 00:27:45.960
So you don't have to worry
about the downtime of your API

00:27:45.960 --> 00:27:47.320
server.

00:27:47.320 --> 00:27:50.220
You don't have to resize your
master control plane just

00:27:50.220 --> 00:27:52.380
because you want to
scale your workload

00:27:52.380 --> 00:27:56.690
or adding more nodes
into your cluster.

00:27:56.690 --> 00:27:59.750
GKE also monitors the
health of your nodes

00:27:59.750 --> 00:28:02.480
and provides auto
repair mechanism

00:28:02.480 --> 00:28:07.880
if you want it and offers
different options for upgrade.

00:28:07.880 --> 00:28:12.160
There's also logging, out
of box logging, monitoring

00:28:12.160 --> 00:28:14.910
integration, and a lot more
integration with other Google

00:28:14.910 --> 00:28:15.535
Cloud Services.

00:28:18.050 --> 00:28:21.080
So we are working very hard
to bring Windows containers

00:28:21.080 --> 00:28:27.560
support to GKE so that you
get to fully experience GKE

00:28:27.560 --> 00:28:30.202
while running both Linux
and Windows workloads.

00:28:33.650 --> 00:28:36.440
So here's a preview of how
Windows Server containers will

00:28:36.440 --> 00:28:38.980
look like on GKE.

00:28:38.980 --> 00:28:40.820
So again, the
architecture diagram--

00:28:40.820 --> 00:28:44.203
but this time, we have
the masters in a cloud.

00:28:44.203 --> 00:28:46.370
So that just means you don't
have to worry about it.

00:28:46.370 --> 00:28:48.203
You don't need to care
about what's actually

00:28:48.203 --> 00:28:50.350
running in there right now.

00:28:50.350 --> 00:28:52.860
And in GKE, there's a concept
called node pool, which

00:28:52.860 --> 00:28:55.020
basically is just
a group of nodes

00:28:55.020 --> 00:28:56.820
with the same
configuration-- for example,

00:28:56.820 --> 00:29:01.230
the same machine type,
the same OS image.

00:29:01.230 --> 00:29:04.890
And so far, we've only been
supporting Linux node pools.

00:29:04.890 --> 00:29:06.680
We'll be adding Windows
node pool support,

00:29:06.680 --> 00:29:10.110
meaning that you could just
add the new Windows node

00:29:10.110 --> 00:29:13.800
pool into an existing cluster.

00:29:13.800 --> 00:29:16.200
And then you can run
mixed Windows and Linux

00:29:16.200 --> 00:29:17.450
workloads in the same cluster.

00:29:22.330 --> 00:29:24.930
So you may be
wondering, but what will

00:29:24.930 --> 00:29:26.970
happen to my Linux workloads?

00:29:26.970 --> 00:29:29.880
Will they get scheduled
onto those Windows node

00:29:29.880 --> 00:29:31.330
and fail to run?

00:29:31.330 --> 00:29:33.330
So to prevent that
from happening,

00:29:33.330 --> 00:29:36.390
we'll be tenting the
Windows node automatically.

00:29:36.390 --> 00:29:39.600
So tent is a common
mechanism used in Kubernetes

00:29:39.600 --> 00:29:41.880
to steer the workflows away.

00:29:41.880 --> 00:29:45.960
So any workflow that cannot
be run on Windows will be

00:29:45.960 --> 00:29:49.830
automatically assigned
to Linux nodes.

00:29:49.830 --> 00:29:51.600
This ensures a
backward compatibility

00:29:51.600 --> 00:29:52.760
for your workloads.

00:29:55.880 --> 00:29:59.840
So if you just want to create
a pod that runs on Windows,

00:29:59.840 --> 00:30:00.830
what would you do?

00:30:00.830 --> 00:30:02.600
So in this case,
all you need to do

00:30:02.600 --> 00:30:06.290
is to add a node selector
to target Windows nodes.

00:30:06.290 --> 00:30:09.590
And in GKE, we will add
the toleration for you

00:30:09.590 --> 00:30:12.680
so that it can get
scheduled onto Windows node.

00:30:12.680 --> 00:30:15.950
And remember that I mentioned
previously that some

00:30:15.950 --> 00:30:19.160
of the features work
for Windows, some don't.

00:30:19.160 --> 00:30:21.500
And it's actually a
very complicated process

00:30:21.500 --> 00:30:23.780
and documentation
to look through.

00:30:23.780 --> 00:30:26.180
To help with our
users, we'll also

00:30:26.180 --> 00:30:29.360
be validating the
fields in the passback,

00:30:29.360 --> 00:30:31.280
making sure that the
features you are using

00:30:31.280 --> 00:30:33.210
are actually supported.

00:30:33.210 --> 00:30:35.930
And if not, we'll
reject right away so

00:30:35.930 --> 00:30:38.060
that you know what's going
on with your workload.

00:30:41.660 --> 00:30:44.630
So enough with the talking,
